WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.363
Perception is a best guess.

2
00:00:03.863 --> 00:00:07.048
Here's a checkerboard, with a cylinder standing on it.

3
00:00:07.178 --> 00:00:09.887
The cylinder casts a shadow across the board.

4
00:00:10.017 --> 00:00:12.263
Look at the two squares marked A and B.

5
00:00:12.963 --> 00:00:14.331
Which one is lighter?

6
00:00:14.461 --> 00:00:16.234
Decide before we go on.

7
00:00:19.934 --> 00:00:21.521
Most people say B.

8
00:00:21.651 --> 00:00:23.255
It looks much lighter than A.

9
00:00:24.055 --> 00:00:27.057
Now let's cover everything except the two squares.

10
00:00:27.187 --> 00:00:30.660
Without the board and the shadow, A and B look the same.

11
00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:32.294
Because they are the same.

12
00:00:32.424 --> 00:00:34.172
Exactly the same grey.

13
00:00:35.472 --> 00:00:36.873
Bring the scene back,

14
00:00:36.723 --> 00:00:39.912
and join the squares with a bar of that same grey.

15
00:00:40.042 --> 00:00:42.005
Follow the bar from A to B.

16
00:00:42.135 --> 00:00:44.578
There's no edge where the colour changes.

17
00:00:45.878 --> 00:00:49.458
Even now that you know, B probably still looks lighter.

18
00:00:49.588 --> 00:00:52.327
Knowing the answer doesn't switch the effect off.

19
00:00:53.627 --> 00:00:57.410
The same amount of light reaches your eye from both squares.

20
00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.102
But your brain sees that B is in shadow.

21
00:01:00.232 --> 00:01:03.565
So it infers that B is a light square in dim light,

22
00:01:03.415 --> 00:01:06.061
and A is a dark square in bright light.

23
00:01:06.191 --> 00:01:09.296
It reports what the surfaces are probably like,

24
00:01:09.146 --> 00:01:11.622
not how much light reaches your eye.

25
00:01:12.922 --> 00:01:14.188
That's perception.

26
00:01:14.318 --> 00:01:16.664
A best guess, built from context,

27
00:01:16.514 --> 00:01:19.592
and from what usually happens in the world.

28
00:01:19.722 --> 00:01:21.984
Usually, it's a very good guess.

29
00:01:23.084 --> 00:01:28.530
Your students make guesses like this whenever they look at a new diagram or graph.

30
00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:30.521
You already know what matters.

31
00:01:30.651 --> 00:01:32.496
Their eyes are still guessing.

32
00:01:32.626 --> 00:01:34.369
So tell them what to look at.

33
00:01:35.669 --> 00:01:41.036
Based on Edward Adelson's checker-shadow illusion, from 1995.
